#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56f7f36b-4e9c-5a86-bd20-ca01d9fce41f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9412" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = (3-x)(y+1)^2.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below?\n\n| $y > -1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<   3$ | $x> 3$ |" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x = 3$ and $y = -1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/df8519853487a7da738b4b3c477b141e00287c67dcb9eb804cbd385c6f43b611.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/4666739c474f85cbf8de7e7ca183206ab7b0bd804816a5504f9c1d97b03956b4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | $(y+1)^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/e789ce41c696b456e0c9d79ba8ce60aba030e454ab614902ea13c8d9ccd02d8c.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<  3$ | $x> 3$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "222d8f84-be89-5bcf-94f3-f309635bbcbf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9414" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y) = (x-2)^2(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y) > 0?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/8e635d2ba2e7ca5721ff8b1dec2055b4ac0c9bcc2eca648848c27190271e92b6.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/1ce934096086de33423910710a010400922ea86c66bbfe0b1bfb0d92291ab59e.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)^2$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(1,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) > 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/70a2ca01e47e91f9d818599886f79b0f00edad0cfd12b080995dfbe6cdf68235.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc8b8288-bf1e-5053-8626-a762fdf8c953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/ccc98a2beb22904a810ba0bd7f6f74b2d38ee58cf62d344f4714f1cf8dcc1aa0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311030} {:answer/id #uuid "250bbb5e-46bf-58d3-aebc-a62377144003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/d4a2bce7ff44e32d9fd4dfbcc892bd473e025d1305cfa40514a0f9892caff622.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311031} {:answer/id #uuid "c6de38eb-1742-563d-b11e-336d0b3e77dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/90ab47aa1a7c9ab58291318337289e1bce08050acaaaf9f4fb7e6a3b74731457.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311032} {:answer/id #uuid "02cbdf42-a499-5410-8a32-db1dccc1eb88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/9edcf28abdd93e2a789fd513605bc31de04913edea476b485555cddcdd07bb76.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311033} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd4430c-edfb-58d2-ab52-875ef5d09c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/d0f7d9eedffeb6955deab3d670b7ecd36203a79f7eab9cbdeaa713be52ed15d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "250bbb5e-46bf-58d3-aebc-a62377144003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/d4a2bce7ff44e32d9fd4dfbcc892bd473e025d1305cfa40514a0f9892caff622.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311031} :answer-field/id #uuid "280f375f-3d0b-5c54-a1d2-1cb50d36feff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dda91e78-ace9-56d2-8473-dec09b0b5a79" :question/math-academy-id "q-121269" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=y(x-1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)>0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/3e5663e03b92fc2be3deea9480f5314cff001cab66bfc7242837f72fbd825b69.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/a143baf42225cd4959987aa1e12abab084e4949092c7c18a1a4a2ec401cf744c.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-1)$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(0,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(0,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(2,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)>0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/fd46cb5d9bd91bc73bd152ca41e55ca32ba8af67870c132c906d63bb32f7c961.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e719b0-3f1e-5844-981e-c1c5b993a824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/8662fe5000674a6824270b52fa8f6be5001ee67e4899930200399e9dd42e17fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311037} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf96160-50a2-5e4f-8949-dcfb1f5dc687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/b64742d37a9699877701703701abed5dee1e2e71aa834abbcd1eb21c8cba6e2b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311038} {:answer/id #uuid "3856fa8d-6de0-53bc-bbf2-3b448b982e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/d1d93faf299aa742b09818d806166de8ae940097d0235bda3bc037dca493ecea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311039} {:answer/id #uuid "980fa975-7b2e-564a-a8f7-9d8e80cf7726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/289dfd9ad3a86b2b9c7bb3f2c559e7139ee392f5e1fa4aa3313c7813ac765b34.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311040} {:answer/id #uuid "50cf12dd-8172-529a-b99f-b11138cd305c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/680d673a27ca48d3aecc84437dbabacf94b9f209bb24bec7991447da51cdcd7a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50cf12dd-8172-529a-b99f-b11138cd305c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/680d673a27ca48d3aecc84437dbabacf94b9f209bb24bec7991447da51cdcd7a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311041} :answer-field/id #uuid "66535629-650a-53e9-a594-e2895762b0ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "744c6cb7-16a3-5ccf-b812-79110a1622ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-121273" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x-3)(y+1)^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\ge 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=3$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/83fcb50be8105d19e044245edd1ad492daad4658ffe4a184ce4bd632dd78e4d4.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/099429ef29700b1eb832aee297b3d237737260a44dd01d6fc516b07710758cce.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-3)$ | $(y+1)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(2,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/15efb1840d7a41f4600d05dcedc0c2ea5149c85e8bc1b502d62130a7da09d1b8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f96356-a023-59f9-8d69-f49c4240ef9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311044} {:answer/id #uuid "be3fc398-b7c1-58e7-8983-f4108d7ec319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311045} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd6c29a-eae2-552c-aa04-eb45d171e246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311046} {:answer/id #uuid "1f62b9f3-2761-5ea3-8a25-a41f0d646d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311047} {:answer/id #uuid "b13e42d4-7e88-514a-93f0-463e89429510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cd6c29a-eae2-552c-aa04-eb45d171e246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311046} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9417f58-a22a-5ed3-90ea-b64c2e08320d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d377a23-4bb2-5df7-a2b0-d0ab6bfc6f59" :question/math-academy-id "q-121277" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=(x+2)^{2}(y-2)^{2}.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y),$ shown below?\n\n| $y>2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<-2$ | $x>-2$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-2$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/1c0457cd5861a8b9af72febb0da0ce390a50910cada39dd1b6f1c61bc5f83810.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/590ec40cbf836d0a492aa5da2a4f70494ccf0d808cf4929f273d148642c4c4d4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+2)^{2}$ | $(y-2)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-3,3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(-1,3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-3,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/0a70fbd50b5c79323d67eac3fbd8bbab68c14578aefa0d918d65314fed1ffce0.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<-2$ | $x>-2$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c321cbe4-40da-5e0d-9052-b847688702ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/91ec230c1251255ad019b445ba18bc35cf46ebf5fceaac74a359ecd819e5ec1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311051} {:answer/id #uuid "a241dcc4-2388-56ac-a8f8-17bd6b943c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/ec94dbaaf4cc906de62faa9c2eea1ca9028915377f18ff5bf4aa5bd8d53b2df2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311052} {:answer/id #uuid "f4357636-1bd7-5d72-95c3-10f069a9ef3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/74cb28a25b133ac6ce3b5abcccef89620350ca901fd60ec66888ed7fee454e18.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311053} {:answer/id #uuid "b348b87a-9431-53d7-9109-508c609b4719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/d996ae0305472b14d52d3ec951669590aad2a249a3ce2f2643d65ad17993a899.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311054} {:answer/id #uuid "d7db3619-e740-581f-852b-067fa3a08854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/10080518e756d9d915dadc4346c23a9db90ff7d0f8df90b09f571b3f9f8aa29c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b348b87a-9431-53d7-9109-508c609b4719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/d996ae0305472b14d52d3ec951669590aad2a249a3ce2f2643d65ad17993a899.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311054} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d258f49-4904-530e-8d9e-5c83d2b74986" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a662f9bf-4050-5954-8495-805c0cc66f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-121310" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x+1)^{2}(y+2).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-1$ and $y=-2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/26f786cb4b0e0d923502a6fef4c827341a5a8c5f61d497ec479742db3ee7ea87.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/0f68fc744e359192c65134f941952d8cfcf928b1f74924896df09ea23a36d07f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+1)^{2}$ | $(y+2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(0,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,-3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(0,-3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/206cc195b55db8f3793f4d84d0bd3b78f21430759770c8be4c815f8db8d562fb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ff67824-88b8-5883-aff3-870c414a4ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/6979eefe00fcb6e1c0e7cb5b993d9b3e162922443d26a6571bd3a86679576d86.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311058} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8442c7-f901-5fcc-812f-b01dc861df1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/b8f4397a5b7dea9e0cbad1c2e61f2a595658dbfad0ecee82e5797ed0431c8ca1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311059} {:answer/id #uuid "4d013b50-3e53-535f-8321-de95ea373285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/28d18defd0b2fbd2565e3929e0acf2575d9ecbe90e931a43e2806616e8041094.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311060} {:answer/id #uuid "f6afe9a6-7ad8-59a0-b7ee-830fa8680a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/b5f63ced598cd8a8a28f774b1d11cf73e340827b2032fd574bc7b9612e4ff467.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311061} {:answer/id #uuid "047374e8-12e0-5c7f-adf7-53d3d311ce14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/8180521b3a878e91950d27111104c0c2ef918ac67dd3ec902fc3861e56270179.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6afe9a6-7ad8-59a0-b7ee-830fa8680a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/b5f63ced598cd8a8a28f774b1d11cf73e340827b2032fd574bc7b9612e4ff467.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311061} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd411ec-af27-5d78-84b8-c2ff358255a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9769f13c-3532-5731-bc38-1e623e8a1641" :question/math-academy-id "q-121313" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{3}(y+3).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)>0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-3.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/6c1830847adf66b3d015227fb2b822d236cc0ca04d4e79b34485e0e307bebfb2.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/e04638efe40ea1813185855bb8c9a47e3e782487f6497ba63c7ed5e9046af421.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)^{3}$ | $(y+3)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(1,-2)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,-2)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-4)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-4)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)>0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/51df420aa99597c5f476be571557aa9f1c91a1ec1f888687058bfd0fe96813e4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3e8113a-e541-5523-9c3a-85702632c730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311065} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7081f2-ee29-517a-8ab1-cba3663d6952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311066} {:answer/id #uuid "595db54d-029c-5b8c-9cf3-6dbcce8946ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311067} {:answer/id #uuid "17a8cb03-d5df-5abb-af23-ae68fb70eb2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311068} {:answer/id #uuid "ec99ccc6-d5e1-53cc-a83c-373f6274e168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17a8cb03-d5df-5abb-af23-ae68fb70eb2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311068} :answer-field/id #uuid "df60aade-9354-5d57-88b7-312f1624cf1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb7ad426-51c5-57d1-8778-32a1d21ccb44" :question/math-academy-id "q-121315" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=-y(x-2).$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/41e876c3444c2fc9a5103c16b9c6c3b34a4c7596082e4c8235a5e86cd57f8570.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/c21e4edde44891c4173420575268750fc0f1f407ba8503953de86a9cc72297dc.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-y$ | $(x-2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/b668aa9647600567c5d6deccbe3830a26561c06f920e2db2364e632f26c39eff.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa196a19-3dad-5f24-8b9f-999ec212d659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311072} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd06188-de1c-58ad-a5dc-c999cbf9859c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311073} {:answer/id #uuid "1724dbc3-b64b-5c20-85b3-ab43cc810569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311074} {:answer/id #uuid "e136d3b1-a9d2-56ff-9aff-912dbb5d8f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311075} {:answer/id #uuid "85e8b5fd-c295-5328-b30b-527ea27c0d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa196a19-3dad-5f24-8b9f-999ec212d659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311072} :answer-field/id #uuid "95ce4a22-8f63-5ca4-af95-0f684e0beccd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e58710c-8293-5127-9aea-2f3fc3bb3b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-121317" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=y(x-3)^{3}.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<3$ | $x>3$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=3$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/6b88b092c69226e8590547f6cf1c27fb5046006c6d24516c8a112ea5ae5fe665.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/9ce50d0e01602f4c28666444df79e9feb98312338b2fe11a2760d7587ea5a548.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $y$ | $(x-3)^{3}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/f03e23b1a5f1bc4faf478d0a14c90d4bc2f24f5e56a61e9708dd7339ff5558e6.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<3$ | $x>3$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d1cad7a-b673-5c45-93ba-7d130b9fc391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311079} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f8afcf-22c3-515d-9a90-7fc4324a481a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311080} {:answer/id #uuid "051e2a0a-b740-5ffa-9b8a-f140254282e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311081} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5d44d1-bb64-52f0-9fca-97455afedfd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311082} {:answer/id #uuid "bedd5892-27ac-5fa1-aa42-fb4729a2c742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bedd5892-27ac-5fa1-aa42-fb4729a2c742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311083} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe48cc3-ae93-5420-9056-c85e6a758e72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fb709d8-90a5-57a1-95a3-b0285559ae32" :question/math-academy-id "q-149548" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(1-x)(y+2)^{2}.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>-2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-2$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=-2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/345665733b6554a7cea9b54a4ca4e93b6d478d67231cfaf3f6180cf0a75c4b1a.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/4058b0f027297faf71756da7cad372b76aae980657f053b95318115889c1d614.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $1-x$ | $(y+2)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(0,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(2,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(0,-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(2,-3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/fc54a4044465d485d4eb55aa8dd81c0e952e8dd56528bc2874bcc67dcffbf3c8.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-2$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-2$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51cdf062-77d3-53fc-b3a0-4ccd9f0d2e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/330f6080a3640f193e3d1650405030c049f059aaa9e3c6f280d2355382ed2e69.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296057} {:answer/id #uuid "109d3ab8-3738-54b4-aee0-77f5d905df8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ff554d3264fe5aeb9cca37a143888c8eb7a7e31f9bcdb3f8512309e45397aeb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296058} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c669d3-d855-58be-8f27-56cead888a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/02a01c1a57b61374e7f9c44b7609f7c0b48c9f91a44ffd0d4a86afeb0909ed68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296059} {:answer/id #uuid "3da33b3f-5b9a-5853-9477-74cbb9fcc973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ba2d7fbeb1a79d32f499e3c3ae81595e4a58fdd4486c6e558fc129fe0e5b3385.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296060} {:answer/id #uuid "d533bd23-d1ab-578e-aee5-b52d4c72a90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/d1958e53fc544743a537eccab139fa33716823e3b57b4aeac90c3b17ec7e8e48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3da33b3f-5b9a-5853-9477-74cbb9fcc973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ba2d7fbeb1a79d32f499e3c3ae81595e4a58fdd4486c6e558fc129fe0e5b3385.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296060} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd4a7138-7632-523f-84fa-1bf20cd8015f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf0f09c4-74cb-5db2-aad0-f5a0e418906f" :question/math-academy-id "q-149553" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=x(2-y).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)>0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/41f10e527834d0767709b965150054a564eaee9c174204381ed94b0e43077fe0.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/994e35e23e56191ef06a155912a326d28d857f236308655265d18d17bb48531b.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | $(2-y)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,3)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)>0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/9710358526e0e14fbf7263ba5d1496cdcb2941f9cb32a1d07b2ea37865163ab2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb8fe931-280f-5c63-a034-f404f93c57a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311086} {:answer/id #uuid "84593211-0c2a-5fe4-b2d3-29dbd7f19dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb8fe931-280f-5c63-a034-f404f93c57a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311086} :answer-field/id #uuid "329fadcf-2ad1-56ef-aa6a-d4f24327f8b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cdec670-c854-5ba0-892f-0f3fc5c8eb98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311089} {:answer/id #uuid "d243be25-710e-542c-9772-d1677491ceb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cdec670-c854-5ba0-892f-0f3fc5c8eb98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311089} :answer-field/id #uuid "76e9652e-738c-5c6d-8642-5e4fd5498a9f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "422500f1-2e3e-53ae-93f7-6764b61fd5ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311092} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f4a14f-29f2-5973-8f2f-66a62557bdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6f4a14f-29f2-5973-8f2f-66a62557bdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311093} :answer-field/id #uuid "b792734a-6f50-5a4e-8e1e-1c37527a1a98" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311091} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f899903-5d14-5849-afc9-ce0d53f1bd88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311095} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce28a7a-777e-5e5e-a5cd-858a0a21c346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ce28a7a-777e-5e5e-a5cd-858a0a21c346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311096} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ba99c95-c293-5d3b-9d30-ae554ef3f07b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "023abe06-191f-5e12-8e21-5b19e4d43ccd" :question/math-academy-id "q-326449" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+1)^{2}(y-2).$ Fill in the missing signs in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $y>2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-1$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/bce3befee4d7ae90df59b7bfb17e965ca29615220ca6845a78cfaad3e02a0730.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/b6cac24748d4a3990038ef7140943bd0947207456132e66ca6b7fb887be44164.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+1)^{2}$ | $(y-2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-2,3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(0,3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(0,1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/174965ef064b55b29326c4681f709a6dfcbc690673e2e5a7498e9a65c4a6af2c.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |"}]}
